  "slug": "condensation-dynamics-e624",
  "summary": "Hi-C-trained and epigenome-informed polymer models now infer chromosome ensembles and locus dynamics directly from contact and epigenomic data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is the use of polymer-based models to infer chromosome organization from experimental contact or epigenomic data. One study builds epigenetic-state-informed polymer models that generate conformational ensembles reproducing Hi-C maps and reveal chromosome folding landscapes across distinct genetic and epigenetic contexts. A second applies a Full-Inversion Chromatin model that learns an effective force field from Hi-C contacts to infer budding yeast chromosome ensembles and locus-dependent dynamics across the cell cycle. A third, a Latent Cell-State Model treating cell-state transitions as a Markov chain, addresses scRNA-seq trajectories rather than chromatin structure and is included only as a methodological neighbor. What is new in the chromatin studies is the inference of unobserved dynamical quantities, such as locus-level dynamics and force fields, from static structural readouts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
